# THE SUITE REGISTRY — every contract suite in this repo, and who runs it.
#
# Format:  <directory>  <harness>
#
# Harnesses:
#   contract-runner           the vendored Haskell Gherkin executor. We are the
#                             PROVIDER: legs 0, 1, 2 and 4 all apply, so its
#                             expectations are executed against the recorded pact.
#   contract-runner-consumer  the same executor, but we are the CONSUMER: these
#                             are our expectations of somebody else's API, which
#                             they run against their own server. Held to totality,
#                             vocabulary and semver here; never executed here,
#                             because we are not the provider and their server is
#                             not ours to start.
#   aqc-dual                  the Atlas Query Contract's own dual harness --
#                             Rust `cucumber` in-process (server/atlas-server/
#                             tests/aqc_cucumber.rs) AND C# Reqnroll
#                             (client.ContractTests). NOT executed by the Haskell
#                             runner: its steps are defined in those two harnesses,
#                             and its corpus uses Gherkin constructs (Scenario
#                             Outline) the vendored parser does not implement.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (fix round 1, review C-2).
#
# The gate used to name its suites in a literal bash array, which is how
# `contracts/atlas-edge` came to be excluded from every tag check -- a one-line
# `@target` could disarm map-generator's expectations of us and nothing objected.
# The fix was to enumerate from the filesystem so nothing could escape. But the
# filesystem alone cannot say WHICH executor owns a suite, and pointing the
# Haskell runner at the AQC corpus produces a wall of "undefined step" reds for a
# suite that is perfectly healthy under its own harness -- caught by
# scripts/gate-selftest.sh on its first run, not by inspection.
#
# So detection stays with the filesystem and classification lives here, and the
# gate FAILS on any suite it finds that this file does not list. Adding a suite
# without registering it is therefore impossible to do quietly, which is the
# property the literal array lacked.
#
# THIS FILE IS A CACHE, NOT AN AUTHORITY (fix round 2, review C-NEW-2).
#
# The paragraph above closed "a suite cannot be ADDED quietly" and left "a suite
# cannot be EXEMPTED quietly" wide open. The reviewer changed one word on the
# atlas-edge row -- contract-runner -> aqc-dual -- and the full gate printed
# PASSED with map-generator's fixture violated AND a plain @target planted: the
# suite simply vanished from every list. The registry had become the literal
# bash array it replaced, still ungated.
#
# WHAT ROUND 2 CLAIMED HERE, AND WHY IT WAS FALSE (fix round 3, review C-R2-2).
#
# The version of this header shipped in round 2 said: "Editing a row below
# therefore cannot exempt anything on its own." It could. The reviewer changed
# `contracts/atlas-graph-contract  contract-runner` to
# `contract-runner-consumer` -- a word with NO derivation guarding it at all --
# and the graph-primary suite, the owner's ruled #1 juncture, left four legs
# with a real break planted in a published export. `-> aqc-dual` plus a
# three-line NOTES.md containing the words "Scenario Outline" did the same,
# because the derivation was `grep -r` over EVERY FILE in the directory.
#
# A false reassurance in a header is worse than silence: a reviewer who reads it
# stops checking. So this one states only what is true today, and each clause
# names the mechanism that makes it true.
#
#   1. THE GATE READS THIS FILE FROM GIT, NOT FROM DISK. contract-gate.sh
#      materialises contracts/ from HEAD and reads the registry out of THAT.
#      Editing the working copy changes nothing whatsoever.
#   2. A ROW MAY ONLY GET STRONGER. contract-semver-gate.sh compares the
#      registry at the base against the registry at HEAD and refuses any row
#      whose harness weakens: contract-runner (executed) > contract-runner-
#      consumer (parsed only) > aqc-dual (run elsewhere) > unregistered. A
#      committed downgrade is a hard failure, not a bump class.
#   3. THE GATE DOES NOT ASK THIS FILE WHAT MUST EXIST. The set of expectations
#      is derived from the committed corpus, and every one must appear in the
#      executor's own results. A row that removes a suite from the run produces
#      MISSING COVERAGE for each of its scenarios; it does not shorten a list.
#   4. `aqc-dual` is accepted only where the vendored parser GENUINELY CANNOT
#      READ the corpus -- tested by running `contract-runner tags` against the
#      committed suite and requiring it to FAIL. A parse failure is a fact no
#      adjacent NOTES.md can manufacture. Such a suite is outside the coverage
#      reconciliation (its own two harnesses execute it), so its committed
#      scenario COUNT is ratcheted instead: it may not fall.
#   5. A directory carrying RECEIVED.md at HEAD MUST be registered
#      contract-runner, and the marker itself is ratcheted -- a suite that had
#      one at the base must still have one at HEAD. Round 2 guarded on the
#      DIRECTORY's existence, so `rm RECEIVED.md` passed while switching off
#      every received-suite protection at once (review C-R2-1).
#
# If you find yourself wanting to weaken a row, the honest move is the one
# RECEIVED.md names: break, report, and coordinate on both sides.

contracts/atlas-graph-contract   contract-runner
contracts/atlas-edge             contract-runner
contracts/map-api-consumer       contract-runner-consumer
contracts/atlas-query-contract   aqc-dual
